title The meter unbraided, and the first hour written from commands actually run
prompt unbraid the fascia meter, then open arc four
think Read the meter whole before touching it and found the braid was entirely in the wrapper. The measurement itself was fine -- tools/fixtures/fascia_metric_v0.sh reports a grade in under six seconds -- and three Amphora laps sat in front of it as a gate.
think Found why lap 2 fails, and it was not a code defect: openssl is absent from this pier. A health meter had been silent for an unknown span on account of a missing binary in an unrelated module, which is the braid stated as plainly as it can be stated.
think Found a second thing on the way in that nobody had asked me to look for. The scan printed amphora_ready=yes while testing only that three FILES existed -- a claim strictly weaker than its own name, and false here, since the lap it declared ready cannot run at all. REDS %110's lesson at a different scale: a line that prints a stronger word than it measures is a red whether or not anyone has noticed.
think Removed both presence bits rather than repairing them. Nothing downstream read them, connective-tissue health does not depend on one module's stack, and eight amphora witnesses already prove Amphora, which is where a claim about Amphora belongs.
think Left lap 2 honestly RED and said so in the closing row rather than letting the unbraid read as a fix. The lap needs openssl installed or its export taught the sha256sum already present, and that is its own round.
think Chose the README's four numbers by what MOVES rather than by what impresses. fascia, witnesses, modules, rooms-over-bound change when something real changes; the commit count and the file count move every commit, say nothing about whether the software is well, and would make the block stale within a day -- which is how a generated block quietly stops being regenerated.
think Hit two Rishi bounds building the renderer and treated both as design rather than obstacle. There is no escape for a newline and no multi-line string, so a real newline comes from printf -- the shell owns character escapes and asking it for the one character needed is smaller than teaching the language a syntax. And Rishi strings are bounded, so an 18 KB README overruns them: the claim stays in Rishi and the byte-shuffling goes to a fixture, which is the honest division rather than a workaround.
think Proved the drift path by building it. A copy of the real README gets one digit changed to a number no measurement will ever produce, and the check must refuse it by name. A generated number is only worth more than a typed one while it is regenerated; left alone it becomes a typed number wearing a generated one's clothes, and trusted MORE for it.
think Refused to write a tutorial command I had not run. Testing found rish_env_witness needs an env var a runner sets, so it would have failed for every beginner who copied it -- the first command in a first hour, failing, is the worst possible first impression. rish_exit_codes_witness runs standalone in 0.05 seconds and prints a green line that means something.
think Tested the five-line program before writing it down, and let its real output stand: 78 rooms. Two of its five lines are assertions, which is the discipline in miniature and worth saying out loud to someone on their first day.
obs Named the roughest edge rather than papering over it. There is no one-command toolchain fetch in this tree, so step two of the first hour is a download done by hand. Every other step is copyable. Writing that down honestly is worth more than inventing a command that does not exist, and it names the next thing worth smoothing.
obs Fixed the em dash in rish_exit_codes_witness rather than quoting it into a new ASCII-first document. The rule repairs on touch, and fixing the source beats fixing the quote -- the tutorial now shows exactly what the tool prints, and both are ASCII.
loom meter=fascia unbraided=yes gates_removed=3 grade=45 of=100 wall_s=5.7 was=silent
loom readme=metrics_block numbers=4 fascia=45 witnesses=1633 modules=1887 rooms_over=0 idempotent=yes drift_refused=yes
loom tutorial=first_hour steps=6 commands_tested=6 commands_untested=0 hand_steps=1 reason=no_toolchain_fetch_command
file tools/gen/season/fascia_metric_v0.rish the three Amphora gates removed; the meter takes its reading directly
file tools/fixtures/fascia_metric_v0.sh the false amphora_ready line removed rather than repaired
file crux/REDS.md %112 CLOSED -- the meter reports 45, and lap 2 is left honestly red with its reason
file tools/readme_metrics.rish renders the four numbers and splices them; newline from printf, bytes to a fixture
file tools/fixtures/readme_metrics_scan.sh the four numbers, and the argued absence of the other two
file tools/fixtures/readme_metrics_check.sh is the block still true against a measurement taken this second
file tools/fixtures/readme_metrics_control.sh a planted drift, so the check is proven to refuse
file tools/readme_metrics_witness.rish true, generated, refuses drift, idempotent
file README.md the generated block between markers, and the first-hour link above the map
file docs-geode/tutorials/the-first-hour.md six steps, every command run before it was written down
recommend check-in (checkpoint) all four arcs are closed and the front door carries a real number. Two doors want a word rather than a kg: the color normalization booked as REDS %114, which edits dated bodies, and a one-command toolchain fetch, which is the roughest edge left on the beginner path and the honest next thing to smooth. Amphora lap 2 stays red on this pier for want of openssl, named in %112's closing row so it is not mistaken for fixed.
